Author: Alexandru Roman <alexandru.roman@uipath.com>
Date:   Sat Sep 5 23:17:44 2026 +0300

    Companion suite: agent imports, sources, setlist, transport, multi-step (#6)
    
    Turns Prompt Player into a coding-agent companion as well as a demo tool, and
    closes five defects found while reviewing main.
    
    Bugs fixed
    - The picker was not capture-excluded on either platform: `prepare_picker`
      lost its only caller in 1d33436. Now applied on both, before `show()`, and
      again at setup before the window is composited.
    - `$SELECTION` and `TM_FILENAME` always expanded to nothing — the contexts
      existed but were never populated, so the shipped refactor example typed a
      prompt with a hole in it. Now read from the Accessibility API (macOS) or UI
      Automation (Windows), and only when the body references them.
    - Choices resolved silently: `${1|a,b|}` always typed its first option. The
      palette now asks inline before it dismisses (§6.4 rules out a modal).
    - `window-title-regex:` scopes could never match on macOS, because
      NSWorkspace exposes no titles and the matcher rejects an empty one.
    - Duplicate triggers were accepted at save and then silently dropped by the
      matcher, so one of the two prompts never fired again. Checked at edit time
      (§2.2), scoped to active prompts to match what the matcher indexes.
    
    Agent companion
    - Import `.claude/commands`, Claude Code skills, Cursor rules and
      Continue/Copilot prompt files; `$ARGUMENTS` becomes a `${1:arguments}` stop.
    - Picker Run mode (Cmd/Ctrl+Enter): type the body, then submit.
    - Per-prompt and per-library newline mode. Terminals deliver Shift+Enter as a
      plain carriage return, so the chat-app default submits an agent prompt at
      its first blank line; imported prompts are stamped `backslash-enter`.
    - Multi-step turns: `<!-- pp:wait 25s -->` splits a prompt into messages that
      are typed, sent, then followed up. The wait is a fixed duration — the app
      cannot see the agent's output — and the kill-switch reaches a parked run.
    - Repo context (`$GIT_BRANCH`, `$REPO_NAME`, `$CWD`, `repo.*`) read from
      `.git/HEAD`, with no subprocess and no console flash on Windows.
    - `git()` in expressions, finally real: triple-gated on config opt-in, a local
      prompt, and a resolvable repo, with read-only subcommands only. `shell()`
      stays unimplemented, deliberately, and says so.
    - Recents: the palette's default order is frecency, not filesystem order,
      plus "save what I just typed" from the hook's existing keystroke ring.
    
    Demo
    - Setlist with a next-cue hotkey and tray rows on both platforms.
    - Pause, resume and re-speed a running playback; the schedule origin rebases
      only on those events, so absolute scheduling stays drift-free between them.
    - False starts: type part of a word, hesitate, delete, retype. Modelled apart
      from typos because it is a thinking artifact — most on Thoughtful CEO, none
      on Fast Presenter.
    - §11 text-field guard, failing open on "unknown" so Electron and terminal
      targets still work; a refusal restores the commit char the hook swallowed.
    - Red tray flash on kill (§2.7), routed through the icon module so it cannot
      wipe the attention badge.
    - Auto-disarm timer, presenter-display placement, and a Windows keyboard-hook
      watchdog mirroring the macOS Accessibility one.
    - Global hotkeys rebind from config without a relaunch.
    
    Shared prompt sources (public GitHub repos, §7.2 `uses:`)
    - One small request resolves the commit; the tarball downloads only when it
      changed, which matters against a 60/hour anonymous budget.
    - Only `.pp.md` is extracted, behind a path-traversal guard, into a staging
      directory that is then swapped in.
    - Trust posture enforced on load, because a remote prompt gets typed into
      whatever has focus: read-only, `hotkey:` dropped, ids namespaced
      `<source>/<stem>`, and disabled until reviewed. Updates are fetched but not
      applied — the panel shows a prompt-level diff and an Apply button — and any
      source operation is refused while the app is armed or playing.
    - A pack can describe itself in `promptplayer-pack.yaml`, including a
      `min-app-version` gate.
    
    Notes
    - `PromptStore`, `ConfigStore` and `UsageStore` take an explicit path or root;
      their tests were racing each other through a process-wide env var.
    - 21 new IPC commands in all three lockstep lists.
    - The two load-bearing guards (duplicate triggers, archive path traversal)
      were verified by reintroducing each defect and watching the tests fail.
    
